<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|notes>

<\body>
  <chapter*|Processes and central limits: constructing limiting observables>

  <notes-abstract|Ordered correlations of bounded observables can determine a
  limiting model with unbounded fields. Starting from spin fluctuations, we
  construct Gaussian unitary groups, recover their self-adjoint generators,
  and explain which notion of convergence the construction provides.>

  <with|font-shape|italic|Observables <math|\<cdot\>> Part VII. Draft, 26 September 2026.>

  <with|font-series|bold|Earlier in this series.> <notes-link|I. The basic idea|self-adjoint-operators.tm>; <notes-link|II. Beyond the real line|self-adjoint-operators-extensions.tm>; <notes-link|III. Quantum mechanics|self-adjoint-operators-applications.tm>; <notes-link|IV. Constructing observables|self-adjoint-operators-convergence.tm>;
  <notes-link|V. From expressions to observables|self-adjoint-operators-practice.tm>; <notes-link|VI. Random matrices and differential operators|self-adjoint-operators-random-matrices.tm>.

  <notes-link|Detailed proofs for Part VII|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm>.

  <label|sec:central-limits>In Part I, an observable was described by all its
  bounded tests. Here that description becomes a construction method:
  correlations of bounded observables in finite systems can determine a
  limiting model whose natural fields are unbounded. The limiting Hilbert
  space, as well as the operators on it, may have to be built from those
  correlations. We first recall how a process is reconstructed, then carry
  out the construction for a quantum central limit.

  The needed background is Stone's theorem in Part I and the probability
  viewpoint of <notes-link|Part II, Section
  4|self-adjoint-operators-extensions.tm#sec:probability>. <notes-link|Part
  III, Section 4|self-adjoint-operators-applications.tm#sec:algebras>
  introduces the Weyl relations and their regular representations, which
  Section 3 uses.

  <with|font-series|bold|Contents.>

  <\enumerate>
    <item> <hlink|From observables to stochastic processes|#sec:processes>

    <item> <hlink|Central limits: bounded spins can produce unbounded
    fields|#sec:spins>

    <item> <hlink|First construct the unitary groups, then take their
    generators|#sec:generators>

    <item> <hlink|A non-commutative continuity theorem for
    observables|#sec:levy>

    <item> <hlink|Other central limits and a route to quantum
    noise|#sec:noise>
  </enumerate>

  <section*|1. From observables to stochastic processes><label|sec:processes>

  A stochastic process is a family of observables <math|X<rsub|t>>
  examined in one state. In the language of Part I, each <math|X<rsub|t>> is
  described by its bounded tests <math|f<around*|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>.
  <hlink|Accardi, Frigerio and
  Lewis|https://doi.org/10.2977/PRIMS/1195184017> take this as the
  definition. A quantum stochastic process consists of a state
  <math|\<omega\>> on an algebra <math|\<cal-A\>> of bounded observables and
  <math|\<ast\>>-homomorphisms <math|j<rsub|t>:\<cal-B\>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-A\>>,
  one for each time. For a real observable one takes
  <math|\<cal-B\>=L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>> and
  <math|j<rsub|t><around*|(|f|)>=f<around*|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>; the framework
  also allows noncommutative source algebras <math|\<cal-B\>>. Ordered
  quantities
  <math|\<omega\><around*|(|j<rsub|t<rsub|1>><around*|(|b<rsub|1>|)>\<cdots\>j<rsub|t<rsub|k>><around*|(|b<rsub|k>|)>|)>>
  replace joint distributions.

  Even classically, the separate laws at each time do not determine a
  process: they leave its temporal correlations unspecified. In the quantum
  setting one must additionally retain the order of the factors. The
  reconstruction theorem starts from precisely this richer collection of data
  and asks whether it comes from a process.

  For an elementary example, let <math|X<rsub|0>> and <math|X<rsub|1>>
  each take values <math|\<pm\>1> with equal probabilities. In one process
  set <math|X<rsub|1>=X<rsub|0>>; in another make the two variables
  independent. Both have the same single-time laws, but
  <math|\<bbb-E\><around*|[|X<rsub|0>*X<rsub|1>|]>> is 1 in the first and 0 in the
  second. A process needs the information that connects its times. In the
  quantum case, ordered correlations supply that information even when a
  classical joint distribution is unavailable.

  For a quantum example, take a single spin in the state with
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|z>> eigenvalue 1, and let
  <math|X<rsub|t>=cos t*\<sigma\><rsub|x>+sin t*\<sigma\><rsub|y>> be the
  Heisenberg evolution of <math|\<sigma\><rsub|x>> generated by
  <math|H=-\<sigma\><rsub|z>/2>. At the times <math|0> and
  <math|\<pi\>/2> we obtain <math|\<sigma\><rsub|x>> and
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|y>>, each taking the values <math|\<pm\>1> with equal
  probabilities, as in the classical example. Since
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|x>*\<sigma\><rsub|y>=i*\<sigma\><rsub|z>>, the
  two-time correlations are
  <math|\<omega\><around*|(|X<rsub|0>*X<rsub|\<pi\>/2>|)>=i> and
  <math|\<omega\><around*|(|X<rsub|\<pi\>/2>*X<rsub|0>|)>=-i>. No pair of
  real classical random variables has a complex product expectation, and the
  order of the factors matters.

  <with|font-series|bold|Reconstruction principle
  (Accardi\UFrigerio\ULewis).> Call a process <em|cyclic> if finite words in
  the represented algebras, applied to the distinguished state vector, span
  a dense subspace. Suppose we are given all ordered correlations
  <math|\<omega\><around*|(|j<rsub|t<rsub|1>><around*|(|b<rsub|1>|)>\<cdots\>j<rsub|t<rsub|k>><around*|(|b<rsub|k>|)>|)>>,
  as functions of the times and the tests. Suppose they are normalized,
  compatible with insertion of units and with the algebra relations at each
  time, and positive: extended linearly to combinations <math|w> of words,
  they satisfy <math|\<omega\><around*|(|w<rsup|\<ast\>>*w|)>\<geqslant\>0>.
  Then they come from a cyclic process, unique up to unitary equivalence; an
  additional normality condition gives the von Neumann algebra version. The
  proof forms a pre-Hilbert space of time-labelled words, uses the
  correlations as inner products, quotients null vectors and completes.
  Multiplication gives the represented observable algebras, and the empty
  word gives the state vector. This is the GNS idea applied to a whole
  process. An arbitrary collection of single-time laws does not suffice.
  The <notes-link|companion states and proves a precise
  version|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#thm:reconstruction>,
  and Section 3 carries out the construction for a Gaussian limit.

  <section*|2. Central limits: bounded spins can produce unbounded
  fields><label|sec:spins>

  Central limit theorems make this viewpoint especially concrete. My
  <hlink|2018 notes, Sheet 4|../store/iam/quantum-probability-ss18/qp-sheet-4.pdf>
  begin with finite-dimensional spin systems whose fluctuations acquire
  Gaussian laws. <hlink|Sheet 5|../store/iam/quantum-probability-ss18/qp-sheet-5.pdf>
  constructs the limiting unitaries, and <hlink|Sheet
  6|../store/iam/quantum-probability-ss18/qp-sheet-6.pdf> recovers their self-adjoint
  generators. The order of these steps is useful: even when every
  approximating observable is bounded, the limiting Gaussian observable
  cannot be bounded if its variance is positive.

  Here is an elementary tensor version of the quantum central limit theorem.
  Let <math|\<rho\>> be a state on a unital <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebra
  <math|\<cal-N\>>; the <math|2\<times\>2> matrices with a vector state
  already give the main example below. Let <math|a> range over a real linear
  space of self-adjoint elements of <math|\<cal-N\>> satisfying
  <math|\<rho\><around*|(|a|)>=0>. On the <math|N>-fold tensor product, write
  <math|a<rsup|<around*|(|k|)>>=I\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>a\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>I>
  for <math|a> placed in the <math|k>-th factor, and define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>=<frac|1|<sqrt|N>><big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|N>a<rsup|<around*|(|k|)>>,
    <space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|N>=\<rho\><rsup|\<otimes\>N>.
  </equation*>

  Each <math|F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>> is bounded, but boundedness at each
  <math|N> does not give a uniform bound. For a spin observable with
  eigenvalues <math|\<pm\>1>, the extreme eigenvalues of its fluctuation
  are <math|\<pm\><sqrt|N>>, so
  <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>=<sqrt|N>>.
  The growing spectral range leaves room for a Gaussian limit with
  unbounded support.

  Different sites commute; two observables at the same site need not. The
  limiting covariance and commutation form are

  <\equation*>
    C<around*|(|a,b|)>=<frac|1|2>\<rho\><around*|(|a b+b a|)>, <space|2em>\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>=-i\<rho\><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|]>|)>=2
    Im \<rho\><around*|(|a b|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\notes-theorem>
    <with|font-series|bold|Tensor quantum central limit theorem.> For any
  finite list <math|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|m>> (with repetitions allowed)
  and real parameters <math|t<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,t<rsub|m>>,

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|N\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<rho\><rsub|N> <around*|(|e<rsup|i
    t<rsub|1>F<rsub|N><around*|(|a<rsub|1>|)>>\<cdots\> e<rsup|i
    t<rsub|m>F<rsub|N><around*|(|a<rsub|m>|)>>|)> =exp<around*|(|-<frac|1|2>C<around*|(|v,v|)>-<frac|i|2><big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>k>
    t<rsub|j>t<rsub|k>\<sigma\><around*|(|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|k>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|v=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>t<rsub|j>a<rsub|j>>. These ordered
  expectations are often called <em|quasi-characteristic functions>. The
  phase remembers the order of the factors. Separate one-variable Gaussian
  limits would lose it.
  </notes-theorem>

  <with|font-series|bold|Example: Pauli matrices.> Let <math|\<cal-N\>> be
  the <math|2\<times\>2> matrices, <math|\<rho\>> the vector state with
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|z>> eigenvalue 1, <math|a=\<sigma\><rsub|x>> and
  <math|b=\<sigma\><rsub|y>>. Both are centred, and
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|x>*\<sigma\><rsub|y>=i*\<sigma\><rsub|z>> gives
  <math|\<rho\><around*|(|a*b|)>=i>. Hence
  <math|C<around*|(|a,a|)>=C<around*|(|b,b|)>=1>,
  <math|C<around*|(|a,b|)>=0> and <math|\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>=2>, and
  the theorem gives

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|N\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<rho\><rsub|N><around*|(|e<rsup|i*s*F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>>*e<rsup|i*t*F<rsub|N><around*|(|b|)>>|)>
    =e<rsup|-<around*|(|s<rsup|2>+t<rsup|2>|)>/2-i*s*t>.
  </equation*>

  Reversing the order changes the sign of the phase. Each fluctuation
  separately becomes a standard Gaussian, while the pair keeps a trace of
  <math|<around*|[|\<sigma\><rsub|x>,\<sigma\><rsub|y>|]>=2*i*\<sigma\><rsub|z>>.
  By contrast, if <math|\<rho\>> is tracial, for instance the normalized
  trace on <math|2\<times\>2> matrices, then <math|\<sigma\>=0>: the tensor
  central limit is a commuting Gaussian family even though the single-site
  observables do not commute.

  <em|Proof sketch, as in Sheet 4.> Tensor independence gives the exact
  identity

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|N><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|m> e<rsup|i
    t<rsub|j>F<rsub|N><around*|(|a<rsub|j>|)>>|)> =<around*|[|\<rho\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>
    e<rsup|i t<rsub|j>a<rsub|j>/<sqrt|N>>|)>|]><rsup|N>.
  </equation*>

  All products are ordered from left to right in increasing <math|j>. The
  expression inside brackets is

  <\equation*>
    1-<frac|1|2N><big|sum><rsub|j>t<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<rho\><around*|(|a<rsub|j><rsup|2>|)>
    -<frac|1|N><big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>k>t<rsub|j>t<rsub|k>
    \<rho\><around*|(|a<rsub|j>a<rsub|k>|)>+O<around*|(|N<rsup|-3/2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Boundedness of the single-site operators controls the Taylor remainder.
  Raising to the <math|N>-th power proves the formula. This illustrates the
  fluctuation-algebra approach of <hlink|Goderis\UVerbeure\UVets|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00341282>;
  <hlink|Jaksic\UPautrat\UPillet|https://jaksic.xyz/papers_pdf/qcltiid.pdf>
  give a general analytic formulation, including convergence for products of
  bounded continuous functions of the fluctuations. The <notes-link|companion
  proves the theorem in
  full|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#thm:tensor-clt>,
  including the construction of the limiting Weyl representation.

  The commutator gives another way to see why a nonclassical limit survives
  the averaging:

  <\equation*>
    <around*|[|F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>,F<rsub|N><around*|(|b|)>|]> =<frac|1|N><big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|N><around*|[|a,b|]><rsup|<around*|(|k|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Its expectation is <math|i\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>> for every <math|N>. Let
  <math|X<rsub|N>> denote this commutator minus <math|i\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>I>.
  Tensor independence and centering give
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N><around*|(|X<rsub|N><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|N>|)>=O<around*|(|N<rsup|-1>|)>>
  (<notes-link|companion|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#prop:commutator>).
  Thus the commutator approaches a scalar in the state's mean-square sense.
  This explains the phase in the limit without claiming convergence of the
  commutators in operator norm.

  The Pauli example shows that the difference is real. Let
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|N>> be the product vector with every spin in the
  positive <math|\<sigma\><rsub|z>> state. The centred commutator is

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|N>=<frac|2*i|N>*<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|N>\<sigma\><rsub|z><rsup|<around*|(|k|)>>-2*i*I,
    <space|1em>X<rsub|N>*\<Omega\><rsub|N>=0,
    <space|1em><around*|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>>=4.
  </equation*>

  Its mean square in this state is already zero at every <math|N>, while
  its operator norm does not decrease. The vector with every spin pointing
  in the opposite direction attains that norm.

  <section*|3. First construct the unitary groups, then take their
  generators><label|sec:generators>

  Section 2 suggests which relations the limiting unitaries should satisfy.
  If the commutator of the fluctuations were exactly the scalar
  <math|i*\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>>, the Baker\UCampbell\UHausdorff
  formula would give
  <math|e<rsup|i*F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>>*e<rsup|i*F<rsub|N><around*|(|b|)>>=e<rsup|-i*\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>/2>*e<rsup|i*F<rsub|N><around*|(|a+b|)>>>;
  it is a scalar only approximately, in the state's mean-square sense. We
  therefore introduce abstract unitaries <math|W<around*|(|a|)>>, indexed by
  the real space of centred observables and standing for the limits of
  <math|e<rsup|i*F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>>>, with relations

  <\equation*>
    W<around*|(|a|)>W<around*|(|b|)>=e<rsup|-i\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>/2>W<around*|(|a+b|)>,
    <space|1em>W<around*|(|a|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=W<around*|(|-a|)>, <space|1em>W<around*|(|0|)>=I.
  </equation*>

  The covariance and the commutation form are compatible, not independent
  choices. For every finite list, the matrix

  <\equation*>
    <around*|[|C<around*|(|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|k>|)>+<frac|i|2>*\<sigma\><around*|(|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|k>|)>|]><rsub|j,k>
    =<around*|[|\<rho\><around*|(|a<rsub|j>*a<rsub|k>|)>|]><rsub|j,k>\<geqslant\>0
  </equation*>

  is positive, since it is the matrix of inner products of the vectors
  <math|a<rsub|j>*\<Omega\>> in the cyclic representation of
  <math|\<rho\>>. For a pair <math|a,b>, the determinant of the
  <math|2\<times\>2> principal submatrix gives the uncertainty inequality
  (<notes-link|companion|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#lem:compatibility>)

  <\equation*>
    C<around*|(|a,a|)>*C<around*|(|b,b|)>-C<around*|(|a,b|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\><frac|\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)><rsup|2>|4>.
  </equation*>

  Thus a positive covariance alone would not suffice for arbitrary
  commutation relations. Our finite systems supply the required
  compatibility automatically. A degenerate commutation form is allowed.
  The formula
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|G><around*|(|W<around*|(|a|)>|)>=e<rsup|-C<around*|(|a,a|)>/2>>
  defines the limiting Gaussian, or <em|quasi-free>, state. Its positivity
  can be obtained directly from the finite systems. Let
  <math|V<rsub|1,N>,\<ldots\>,V<rsub|n,N>> be words in the fluctuation
  unitaries <math|e<rsup|i*t*F<rsub|N><around*|(|a|)>>>. The matrix of
  expectations
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N><around*|(|V<rsub|j,N><rsup|\<ast\>>*V<rsub|k,N>|)>>
  is positive for every <math|N>, and so is its limit. The resulting GNS
  construction is the reconstruction of Section 1, applied to a single
  algebra of Weyl unitaries.

  In its cyclic representation <math|<around*|(|H<rsub|G>,\<pi\><rsub|G>,\<Omega\><rsub|G>|)>>,
  set <math|U<rsub|a><around*|(|t|)>=\<pi\><rsub|G><around*|(|W<around*|(|t a|)>|)>>. The Weyl relations give a
  one-parameter unitary group for each <math|a>. On the state vector the
  Gaussian formula gives the explicit continuity estimate

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|(|U<rsub|a><around*|(|t|)>-I|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|G>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>
    =2*<around*|(|1-e<rsup|-t<rsup|2>*C<around*|(|a,a|)>/2>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0
    <space|1em><around*|(|t\<rightarrow\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  This alone concerns one vector. The Weyl relations give the same
  continuity on each Weyl-word vector: conjugation adds only a continuous
  scalar phase to the Gaussian expectation. Finite linear combinations of
  these vectors are dense. Since <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|a><around*|(|t|)>-I|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2>,
  approximation extends continuity to every vector. Stone's theorem
  therefore gives a unique self-adjoint operator <math|\<phi\><around*|(|a|)>> with

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|a><around*|(|t|)>=e<rsup|i t \<phi\><around*|(|a|)>>, <space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|G><around*|(|e<rsup|i
    t \<phi\><around*|(|a|)>>|)> =e<rsup|-t<rsup|2>C<around*|(|a,a|)>/2>.
  </equation*>

  Its domain is the domain of strong differentiation of this group, as in
  <notes-link|Theorem 3 in Part I|self-adjoint-operators.tm#thm:stone>. Its
  bounded functional calculus is then determined. If <math|C<around*|(|a,a|)>\<gtr\>0>,
  its vector spectral law is a Gaussian of full support on <math|\<bbb-R\>>,
  so <math|\<phi\><around*|(|a|)>> is necessarily unbounded. The bounded data have
  supplied both the limiting Hilbert space and the domains of its generators.
  Retaining the ordered unitary correlations determines the whole cyclic
  family up to unitary equivalence, including the relations between different
  observables.

  For the Pauli example of Section 2, the limiting fields satisfy

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|G><around*|(|e<rsup|i*s*\<phi\><around*|(|a|)>>*e<rsup|i*t*\<phi\><around*|(|b|)>>|)>
    =e<rsup|-<around*|(|s<rsup|2>+t<rsup|2>|)>/2-i*s*t>.
  </equation*>

  The uncertainty inequality is saturated here:
  <math|1\<cdot\>1-0<rsup|2>=2<rsup|2>/4>. Two-dimensional spins have
  produced unbounded canonical Gaussian fields.

  <section*|4. A non-commutative continuity theorem for
  observables><label|sec:levy>

  The passage from exponentials to other bounded functions has a precise
  theorem behind it. Let <math|A<rsub|N,1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|N,d>> be
  self-adjoint operators affiliated with von Neumann algebras
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|N>> carrying normal states <math|\<omega\><rsub|N>>;
  include a proposed limiting family with <math|N=\<infty\>>. Their Hilbert
  spaces may differ.

  <\notes-theorem>
    <with|font-series|bold|Non-commutative Lévy\UCramér theorem
  (Jaksic\UPautrat\UPillet).> Suppose the expectations of every ordered word
  <math|e<rsup|i t<rsub|1>A<rsub|N,j<rsub|1>>>\<cdots\> e<rsup|i
  t<rsub|m>A<rsub|N,j<rsub|m>>>> converge to those of the limiting family,
  for all word lengths, indices and real parameters. Then, for all
  <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|m>\<in\>C<rsub|b><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|N\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<omega\><rsub|N>
    <around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|N,j<rsub|1>>|)>\<cdots\>f<rsub|m><around*|(|A<rsub|N,j<rsub|m>>|)>|)>
    =\<omega\><rsub|\<infty\>> <around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|\<infty\>,j<rsub|1>>|)>\<cdots\>f<rsub|m><around*|(|A<rsub|\<infty\>,j<rsub|m>>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>
  </notes-theorem>

  <em|Proof idea.> Fourier integration first treats smooth test functions.
  Approximation and the state Cauchy\USchwarz inequality then extend the
  result to bounded continuous functions. In an ordered product, the factors
  to the right of a given test act first on the state vector and produce a
  new vector; that test must be controlled in the spectral law of the new
  vector, not only in the original state. These vectors are approximated by
  combinations of unitary words applied to the state vector, which is why
  all words, including repeated indices, enter the hypothesis. The
  <notes-link|companion carries out this
  argument|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#thm:levy-cramer>;
  the <hlink|original paper|https://jaksic.xyz/papers_pdf/qlct.pdf> also
  treats discontinuous Borel tests.

  In the fluctuation example, this theorem gives convergence of ordered
  products of bounded tests of the observables to the corresponding products
  for the limiting Gaussian fields. In particular, it gives convergence of
  mixed resolvent expectations. It does not assert strong resolvent
  convergence of the operators: the natural limiting Hilbert space was
  constructed anew. With a specified common Hilbert space and strong
  convergence of the unitary groups, <notes-link|Theorem 5 of Part
  I|self-adjoint-operators.tm#thm:convergence> does give strong resolvent
  convergence. Convergence of expectations in a single state is a different
  assertion.

  One must also distinguish the limit construction from the continuity
  theorem: the theorem assumes a limiting self-adjoint family. Above,
  positivity of the limiting word correlations constructs its Hilbert space,
  continuity makes its unitary groups regular, and Stone's theorem supplies
  that family. Mere pointwise limits without this regularity need not provide
  generators. <notes-link|Part III, Section
  4|self-adjoint-operators-applications.tm#sec:algebras> explains why
  regularity lives in the representation and is not norm continuity in the
  abstract Weyl algebra.

  <section*|5. Other central limits and a route to quantum
  noise><label|sec:noise>

  The literature contains several meanings of non-commutative central limit.

  <subsection*|Canonical variables with finite second moments>

  <hlink|Cushen\UHudson (1971)|https://doi.org/10.2307/3212170> start with
  independent copies of canonical position\Umomentum pairs, whose entries are
  already unbounded. With zero means and finite covariances, their normalized
  sums converge to a Gaussian quantum state. Characteristic functions of Weyl
  unitaries identify this limit. This is closely related to the bounded-spin
  fluctuation construction, but the hypotheses now concern a normal state in
  a regular representation of the canonical commutation relations, and
  finite second moments replace boundedness in the second-order expansion of
  the characteristic function. The <notes-link|companion gives the
  argument|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#prop:canonical-clt>.

  Covariances here must be read as forms. In the normal cyclic
  representation with state vector <math|\<Omega\>>, finite second
  moments mean <math|\<Omega\>\<in\>D<around*|(|X|)>\<cap\>D<around*|(|Y|)>>, and the centred
  covariance is

  <\equation*>
    C<around*|(|X,Y|)>=<text|Re><around*|\<langle\>|X*\<Omega\>,Y*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Neither <math|X*Y*\<Omega\>> nor <math|Y*X*\<Omega\>> need exist.
  Writing the covariance as an expectation of a product would therefore
  impose an extra domain assumption that this theorem does not need.

  <subsection*|Time-indexed fluctuations and quantum noise>

  Time-indexed sums give a process version. Fix a time horizon
  <math|T\<gtr\>0>. At level <math|N>, use
  <math|K<rsub|N>=<around*|\<lceil\>|N*T|\<rceil\>>> tensor sites in the
  product state <math|\<rho\><rsup|\<otimes\>K<rsub|N>>>. For
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>T>, let

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|N><around*|(|s,a|)>=<frac|1|<sqrt|N>> <big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|<around*|\<lfloor\>|N
    s|\<rfloor\>>>a<rsup|<around*|(|k|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Partitioning the sites into disjoint time blocks and applying the same
  calculation gives finite-dimensional unitary correlations with covariance
  <math|min<around*|(|s,t|)>C<around*|(|a,b|)>> and commutation form <math|min<around*|(|s,t|)>\<sigma\><around*|(|a,b|)>>.
  The limiting generators describe quantum Gaussian noise; their increments
  on disjoint time intervals commute and are independent in the Gaussian
  state. For the Pauli data of Section 2, let <math|Q<rsub|t>> and
  <math|P<rsub|t>> be the limiting generators in the directions
  <math|<around*|(|t,\<sigma\><rsub|x>|)>> and
  <math|<around*|(|t,\<sigma\><rsub|y>|)>>. Each is Gaussian with variance
  <math|t> in the limiting state, and in Weyl form they satisfy
  <math|<around*|[|Q<rsub|s>,P<rsub|t>|]>=2*i*min<around*|(|s,t|)>>: a pair
  of canonical Brownian motions, the simplest quantum noise. The
  <notes-link|companion|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#par:time-indexed>
  gives the block calculation. These limiting correlations also fit the process-reconstruction
  language of Accardi\UFrigerio\ULewis. A full functional limit theorem
  requires a specified notion of process convergence and further estimates;
  <hlink|Cockroft\UGudder\UHudson (1977)|https://doi.org/10.1016/0047-259X(77)90036-7>
  give a quantum analogue of the Donsker theorem for canonical pairs.

  <subsection*|Free independence gives a different limit>

  By contrast, <em|free independence> requires a product of centred factors
  to have expectation zero whenever consecutive factors come from different
  members of the family of free subalgebras. For freely independent,
  identically distributed bounded self-adjoint variables of mean zero and
  variance one, Voiculescu's free central limit theorem gives the semicircle
  law

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2\<pi\>><sqrt|4-x<rsup|2>>1<rsub|<around*|[|-2,2|]>><around*|(|x|)>\<mathd\>x.
  </equation*>

  The proof keeps noncrossing pairings in the moment expansion; ordinary
  tensor independence keeps all pairings. For the fourth moment, three
  pairings of four points exist and only two are noncrossing, so the
  semicircle has fourth moment 2 while the Gaussian has 3. The semicircular
  limit has a bounded realization of norm 2, so unboundedness is not a
  feature of every non-commutative central limit. See <hlink|Speicher's
  lectures, Theorem
  2.8|https://rolandspeicher.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/free-probability_v2.pdf>;
  the <notes-link|companion gives a self-contained
  proof|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm#thm:free-clt>.
  These examples show why the word independence must be specified. Tensor and
  free independence keep different terms in the fluctuation calculation and
  lead to different laws. The functional calculus provides a common language
  for identifying the resulting observables; it does not decide the
  probabilistic structure that produces them.

  <with|font-series|bold|Takeaways.> Ordered correlations of bounded
  observables are enough data to build a limiting model. Positivity gives a
  Hilbert space by the GNS construction, continuity of the limiting
  correlations makes the unitary groups regular, and Stone's theorem then
  supplies self-adjoint generators, unbounded as soon as their variance is
  positive. The resulting convergence concerns ordered bounded tests in the
  states; it is not strong resolvent convergence on a common space. Which
  limit appears is decided by the notion of independence, not by the
  functional calculus.

  <with|font-series|bold|Further reading.> <notes-link|Proofs and complements
  for this part|self-adjoint-operators-central-limits-proofs.tm> supply the
  detailed reconstruction and limit arguments. For operators given by
  formal expressions rather than by limits, see <notes-link|Observables V:
  From expressions to observables|self-adjoint-operators-practice.tm>.

  <section*|References and further reading><label|sec:references>

  <\itemize>
    <item>M. Gubinelli, <em|Quantum probability and quantum stochastic
    calculus> (Bonn, 2018), <hlink|Sheet 6, Section 1, pp.
    1\U6|../store/iam/quantum-probability-ss18/qp-sheet-6.pdf>. The three
    descriptions of a quantum random variable, Fourier reconstruction, the
    generator domain, and the Cayley transform. See also <hlink|Sheet 4,
    Section 2|../store/iam/quantum-probability-ss18/qp-sheet-4.pdf> and
    <hlink|Sheet 5|../store/iam/quantum-probability-ss18/qp-sheet-5.pdf> for
    spin central limits, quasi-characteristic functions and the construction
    of the limiting Gaussian unitaries. <notes-link|Course
    page|../src/teaching/lectures-quantum-probability-ss18.tm>.

    <item>M. Reed and B. Simon, <em|Methods of Modern Mathematical Physics,
    I: Functional Analysis>, revised and enlarged edition, Academic Press,
    1980, especially Chapters VII\UVIII. <hlink|Publisher|https://www.sciencedirect.com/book/9780125850018/methods-of-modern-mathematical-physics>.
    A systematic account of spectral theory and unbounded operators.

    <item>L. Accardi, A. Frigerio and J. T. Lewis, <em|Quantum Stochastic
    Processes>, Publications of the Research Institute for Mathematical
    Sciences <with|font-series|bold|18> (1982), 97\U133. See Section 1,
    especially Theorem 1.3 and the commentary in Section 1.6.
    <hlink|doi:10.2977/PRIMS/1195184017|https://doi.org/10.2977/PRIMS/1195184017>.

    <item>C. D. Cushen and R. L. Hudson, <em|A quantum-mechanical central
    limit theorem>, Journal of Applied Probability <with|font-series|bold|8>
    (1971), 454\U469. <hlink|doi:10.2307/3212170|https://doi.org/10.2307/3212170>.
    A Gaussian limit theorem for canonical pairs, using quantum
    characteristic functions.

    <item>N. Giri and W. von Waldenfels, <em|An algebraic version of the
    central limit theorem>, Zeitschrift für Wahrscheinlichkeitstheorie und
    Verwandte Gebiete <with|font-series|bold|42> (1978), 129\U134.
    <hlink|doi:10.1007/BF00536048|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00536048>. D.
    Goderis, A. Verbeure and P. Vets, <em|Non-commutative central limits>,
    Probability Theory and Related Fields <with|font-series|bold|82> (1989),
    527\U544. <hlink|doi:10.1007/BF00341282|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00341282>.
    Algebraic central limits and the CCR algebra of fluctuations.

    <item>V. Jaksic, Y. Pautrat and C.-A. Pillet, <em|A quantum central limit
    theorem for sums of independent identically distributed random
    variables>, Journal of Mathematical Physics <with|font-series|bold|51>
    (2010), 015208. <hlink|doi:10.1063/1.3285287|https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3285287>;
    <hlink|author's preprint|https://jaksic.xyz/papers_pdf/qcltiid.pdf>.
    Theorem 4.1 and Lemma 5.1 in the preprint give the bounded-calculus limit
    and the ordered-exponential calculation.

    <item>V. Jaksic, Y. Pautrat and C.-A. Pillet, <em|A non-commutative
    Lévy\UCramér continuity theorem>, Markov Processes and Related Fields
    <with|font-series|bold|16> (2010), 59\U78. <hlink|author's
    preprint|https://jaksic.xyz/papers_pdf/qlct.pdf>. Theorem 1 and Section 3
    explain how convergence of all ordered unitary correlations gives
    convergence of bounded continuous calculi; Section 4 discusses examples
    and discontinuous tests.

    <item>A. M. Cockroft, S. P. Gudder and R. L. Hudson, <em|A
    quantum-mechanical functional central limit theorem>, Journal of
    Multivariate Analysis <with|font-series|bold|7> (1977), 125\U148.
    <hlink|doi:10.1016/0047-259X(77)90036-7|https://doi.org/10.1016/0047-259X(77)90036-7>.
    Canonical Wiener processes and a quantum Donsker theorem.

    <item>R. Speicher, <em|Free Probability Theory>, lecture notes for winter
    2018/19, version 2 (April 2025), Theorem 2.8. <hlink|Author's
    notes|https://rolandspeicher.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/free-probability_v2.pdf>.
    The free central limit theorem and its noncrossing-pairing proof.
  </itemize>
</body>

